12
00:00:39,039 --> 00:00:42,000
My name is Christian Kaiser.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,719
I am professor at University of Albstadt-Siegma-Wing in the textile department.

14
00:00:48,719 --> 00:00:53,920
We are educating textile and garment engineers.

15
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:58,980
My scientific background is that I am a

16
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:08,400
engineering cybernetician, which means actually that I'm a control engineer or systems engineer

17
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:18,100
and I got in touch with the textile topics, during my studies at the University of Stuttgart.

18
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:25,659
What I would like to give you a short overview and some insights is how our university introduced

19
00:01:25,659 --> 00:01:28,659
glow3D in our curriculum.

20
00:01:28,659 --> 00:01:39,659
Because before I joined Albstadt-Siedmaring University in 2018, 3D was no focus point at our teaching classes.

21
00:01:39,659 --> 00:01:52,659
My personal background, I'm already 3D user since 2010, so I would say one of the first users in Europe.

22
00:01:52,659 --> 00:01:58,659
And actually, as I'm not from garment and textile industry, it was not hard to convince myself,

23
00:01:58,659 --> 00:02:06,659
because if you go to mechanical engineering, aerospace and all the other mechanical engineering disciplines,

24
00:02:06,659 --> 00:02:11,659
3D development and 3D simulation is state of the art.

25
00:02:11,659 --> 00:02:20,659
So to me, it's pretty obvious that 3D development and 3D visualization of garments is also something

26
00:02:20,659 --> 00:02:22,659
which should be implemented in the future.

27
00:02:22,659 --> 00:02:28,659
And I hope that we can do that in the textile industry as soon as possible.

28
00:02:28,659 --> 00:02:41,659
Starting with what we did to implement 3D classes based on CLO at our bachelor's degree,

29
00:02:41,659 --> 00:02:50,659
the starting point was the winter semester in 2019 to 2020.

30
00:02:50,659 --> 00:02:52,659
There, we started introducing 3D models.

31
00:02:52,659 --> 00:02:56,659
We started using CLO 3D within our bachelor courses.

32
00:02:56,659 --> 00:03:01,659
Actually, at this time, it was starting with one course.

33
00:03:01,659 --> 00:03:06,659
And pretty soon, a colleague of mine, which is a designer, and me,

34
00:03:06,659 --> 00:03:13,659
we entered the academic workshop of CLO in Munich, Germany.

35
00:03:13,659 --> 00:03:20,659
This was our start, where we did refine our

36
00:03:20,659 --> 00:03:22,659
already pre-existing,

37
00:03:22,659 --> 00:03:27,659
understanding of 3D simulation with this type of software.

38
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:31,659
Both of us were skilled in terms of other softwares,

39
00:03:31,659 --> 00:03:35,659
because we are using multiple 3D softwares at our university.

40
00:03:35,659 --> 00:03:38,659
But this workshop was really some kind of game changer,

41
00:03:38,659 --> 00:03:44,659
because after that, we could really speed up our tutorial database,

42
00:03:44,659 --> 00:03:50,659
our online courses, as well as enhance the quality of our presence teaching.

43
00:03:50,659 --> 00:04:01,659
Pretty soon after this, we also had our first students 3D project in 2020,

44
00:04:01,659 --> 00:04:04,659
and our second one just the next semester.

45
00:04:04,659 --> 00:04:11,659
And after this curriculum, we started optimizing our tutorial database.

46
00:04:11,659 --> 00:04:16,659
Perhaps something I should tell you, because I'm a control engineer,

47
00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:18,659
I personally live in a feedback loop,

48
00:04:18,659 --> 00:04:19,659
which means,

49
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:24,660
every kind of teaching, every type of action I'm responsible for,

50
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,660
means we start with some kind of steering impulse,

51
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:31,660
which means like starting with curriculum and stuff like this.

52
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:36,660
And we optimize each of our lectures on a regular basis,

53
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:38,660
based on the feedback of the students,

54
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:41,660
based on proceedings in the market,

55
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:47,660
be it software-wise or be it more application-wise.

56
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:52,660
And this means that mid of 2021,

57
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:55,660
we had our first big run-through,

58
00:04:55,660 --> 00:04:58,660
which we used to optimize our tutorial database

59
00:04:58,660 --> 00:05:06,660
and also go for a second academic workshop at CLO in Munich.

60
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:12,660
In fact, for us, the second workshop wasn't that necessary anymore,

61
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:15,660
because we were pretty skilled in what we were doing,

62
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:16,660
but twofold.

63
00:05:16,660 --> 00:05:18,660
This was super helpful.

64
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:20,660
The first part is networking.

65
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:23,660
I guess all of you experienced that 3D,

66
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:28,660
unless it's not yet that new anymore,

67
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:34,660
is still some area of teaching where we are lacking routine.

68
00:05:34,660 --> 00:05:37,660
The other part is there are super active players,

69
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,660
super skilled players.

70
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:41,660
And if you want to push forward,

71
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:45,660
it's super helpful to build up your own network.

72
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:47,660
Being it in industry,

73
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:49,660
being it in the academic world.

74
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:57,660
And this is the reason why we entered the CLO academic workshop in 2021.

75
00:05:57,660 --> 00:06:02,660
Once again, because this also helped us to go next level.

76
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:07,660
What were the challenges that we were facing?

77
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:14,660
The main challenge is to embed 3D in current curricula.

78
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:17,660
Because I guess all of you experienced this issue.

79
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:21,660
You are having current lessons.

80
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:28,660
And you have to focus in the areas that are important to you.

81
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:31,660
Be it the sewing parts,

82
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:35,660
being it the textile parts,

83
00:06:35,660 --> 00:06:37,660
being it in the CAD,

84
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:39,660
being it as 3D,

85
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:42,660
which is something new entering the curriculum,

86
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:43,660
which means you really,

87
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:45,660
really have to focus.

88
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:47,660
For us,

89
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:50,660
the general focus points were,

90
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:52,660
first of all,

91
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:53,660
the mechanics,

92
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:54,660
the digital sewing,

93
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:57,660
and then going to two application areas,

94
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:00,660
which are the virtual fit and the 3D design part.

95
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:09,660
What you are seeing on the right side is work of my students in 2021,

96
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:11,660
where they,

97
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:13,660
based on the materials,

98
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:15,660
I was teaching them,

99
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:19,660
had to experience their own small projects,

100
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:22,660
like developing a pattern in 2D,

101
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:24,660
transferring the data.

102
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:25,660
In this case,

103
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:29,660
we were using Electra's 2D system to CLO,

104
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:31,660
to arrange patterns,

105
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:33,660
to do the first swings,

106
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:35,660
and then debugging,

107
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:39,660
like optimize the braping of the garment,

108
00:07:39,660 --> 00:07:42,660
optimize the pattern sizes to the fit.

109
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:45,660
And also identify,

110
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:49,660
this is something you're seeing on the shirt of Gabriela Schön,

111
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:51,660
summer semester 2020,

112
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,660
on the backside,

113
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:57,660
which areas are facing inconsistencies,

114
00:07:57,660 --> 00:07:59,660
or that's the other part.

115
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,660
We are linking this also to our sewing classes,

116
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:07,660
are equivalent to what you were facing in the real life.

117
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:12,660
I already talked about embedding 3D.

118
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:18,660
And I really do choose this word by purpose,

119
00:08:18,660 --> 00:08:23,660
which means embedding is something different to a add-on.

120
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:30,660
What I face in European teaching is that many universities

121
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:37,660
understand 3D skills as add-ons to the traditional textile and garment skills.

122
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:39,660
In my opinion,

123
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:42,660
this point of view is pretty mistaken.

124
00:08:42,659 --> 00:08:44,659
It's not an add-on.

125
00:08:44,659 --> 00:08:46,659
It's just a basic skill.

126
00:08:46,659 --> 00:08:50,659
So like the students are able to sew,

127
00:08:50,659 --> 00:08:56,659
like the students are able to develop patterns by hand,

128
00:08:56,659 --> 00:08:59,659
and build up other competencies,

129
00:08:59,659 --> 00:09:02,659
3D is the standard skill.

130
00:09:02,659 --> 00:09:07,659
This means that our students do not have any choice

131
00:09:07,659 --> 00:09:11,659
when it comes to the 3D classes for a pretty long time.

132
00:09:11,659 --> 00:09:12,659
The skills,

133
00:09:12,659 --> 00:09:15,659
we really want to transfer it to our students.

134
00:09:15,659 --> 00:09:19,659
This means that in the bachelor degree,

135
00:09:19,659 --> 00:09:23,659
we are having the digital construction lessons

136
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:26,659
that split between pattern,

137
00:09:26,659 --> 00:09:29,659
pattern means construction by hand,

138
00:09:29,659 --> 00:09:30,659
CAD,

139
00:09:30,659 --> 00:09:35,659
which means construction by computer-aided design,

140
00:09:35,659 --> 00:09:41,659
and the 3D simulation of exactly these CAD patterns.

141
00:09:41,659 --> 00:09:45,659
This allows the students to have a close feedback loop.

142
00:09:45,659 --> 00:09:47,659
What I develop by hand,

143
00:09:47,659 --> 00:09:50,659
I see how to do this on the computer,

144
00:09:50,659 --> 00:09:57,659
and I see the result of the drape in 3D on a defined human model.

145
00:09:57,659 --> 00:10:02,659
We do this like on a standard level in digital construction 2

146
00:10:02,659 --> 00:10:09,659
and go in more advanced 2D and 3D skills in digital construction 3.

147
00:10:09,659 --> 00:10:13,659
In digital construction 4,

148
00:10:13,659 --> 00:10:16,659
we do a shift of focus.

149
00:10:16,659 --> 00:10:19,659
There we go on high-end simulation and rendering

150
00:10:19,659 --> 00:10:23,659
because having the digital twins of your garments

151
00:10:23,659 --> 00:10:26,659
on the technical part is the one thing,

152
00:10:26,659 --> 00:10:29,659
enabling visual communication

153
00:10:29,659 --> 00:10:34,659
and high-end visual communication is the other super important part.

154
00:10:34,659 --> 00:10:38,659
Having this so far enables

155
00:10:38,659 --> 00:10:43,659
the students to use 3D as a standard skill in projects.

156
00:10:43,659 --> 00:10:47,659
These projects might be project lectures,

157
00:10:47,659 --> 00:10:51,659
but might also be projects they are having in different lectures.

158
00:10:51,659 --> 00:10:52,659
For example,

159
00:10:52,659 --> 00:10:55,659
we are having lectures

160
00:10:55,659 --> 00:11:01,659
facing on more uncommon sewing and tufting solutions

161
00:11:01,659 --> 00:11:02,659
like welding,

162
00:11:02,659 --> 00:11:04,659
like gluing and stuff like this,

163
00:11:04,659 --> 00:11:05,659
where the students are,

164
00:11:05,659 --> 00:11:06,659
for example,

165
00:11:06,659 --> 00:11:07,659
in rain jackets.

166
00:11:07,659 --> 00:11:08,659
And

167
00:11:08,659 --> 00:11:13,659
since we started implementing this type of curriculum,

168
00:11:13,659 --> 00:11:19,659
the students use 3D for 3D prototyping

169
00:11:19,659 --> 00:11:26,659
of the garments that they are developing on this lecture on their own.

170
00:11:26,659 --> 00:11:31,659
So this means 3D is a real standard skill.

171
00:11:31,659 --> 00:11:34,659
And the professor having this lecture,

172
00:11:34,659 --> 00:11:36,659
he is actually no 3D expert.

173
00:11:36,659 --> 00:11:38,659
He also did not foster

174
00:11:38,659 --> 00:11:40,659
the students to do so,

175
00:11:40,659 --> 00:11:44,659
but it was the choice of the students on their own to do that.

176
00:11:44,659 --> 00:11:47,659
And this is something which makes me really proud

177
00:11:47,659 --> 00:11:52,659
because regularly you try to push industry exactly in this way,

178
00:11:52,659 --> 00:11:55,659
like a industry garment manufacturer,

179
00:11:55,659 --> 00:11:59,659
please do first of all digital prototyping

180
00:11:59,659 --> 00:12:03,659
before you go to the real prototype.

181
00:12:03,659 --> 00:12:06,659
And that's something that my students,

182
00:12:06,659 --> 00:12:09,659
they really do this on their own purpose.

183
00:12:09,659 --> 00:12:10,659
For sure,

184
00:12:10,659 --> 00:12:15,659
I have to admit that for the lectures that were in advance,

185
00:12:15,659 --> 00:12:16,659
we really,

186
00:12:16,659 --> 00:12:18,659
really did push the students

187
00:12:18,659 --> 00:12:22,659
and we really did raise awareness when it comes to this.

188
00:12:22,659 --> 00:12:24,659
But

189
00:12:24,659 --> 00:12:25,659
in the meantime,

190
00:12:25,659 --> 00:12:31,659
it's still the students adopting this and using these skills.

191
00:12:31,659 --> 00:12:32,659
And

192
00:12:32,659 --> 00:12:35,659
I would so far say 3D is a

193
00:12:35,659 --> 00:12:38,659
standard skill

194
00:12:38,659 --> 00:12:39,659
